        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Multiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Multiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (<strong>product</strong>) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (\u2212)\u22c5(+) = (\u2212)or(+)\u22c5(\u2212) = (\u2212).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "The first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (product) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n[MATH: (\u2212)\u22c5(+) = (\u2212)or(+)\u22c5(\u2212) = (\u2212).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For instance, let&#x27;s find out the value of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-3) \\cdot 10.]</span>\nHere, we have a product of a negative number <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\color{red}(-3)]</span> and a positive one <span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}(10)}.]</span> So the product will be negative:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 30]</span>\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3]</span> by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 10]</span> without any signs: <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3 \\cdot 10 = 30.]</span> So, we get\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "For instance, let's find out the value of [MATH: (-3) \\cdot 10.]\nHere, we have a product of a negative number [MATH: \\color{red}(-3)] and a positive one [MATH: {\\color{blue}(10)}.] So the product will be negative:\n[MATH: 30]\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply [MATH: 3] by [MATH: 10] without any signs: [MATH: 3 \\cdot 10 = 30.] So, we get\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Note that multiplication is <strong>commutative</strong>, meaning it doesn&#x27;t matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we&#x27;re allowed to swap the numbers, which gives</p>",
            "readable_text": "Note that multiplication is commutative, meaning it doesn't matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we're allowed to swap the numbers, which gives",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} =  {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)}  = {\\color{red} -30 }.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nMultiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.\n\nContent:\nThe first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (product) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n[MATH: (\u2212)\u22c5(+) = (\u2212)or(+)\u22c5(\u2212) = (\u2212).]\n\nContent:\nFor instance, let's find out the value of [MATH: (-3) \\cdot 10.]\nHere, we have a product of a negative number [MATH: \\color{red}(-3)] and a positive one [MATH: {\\color{blue}(10)}.] So the product will be negative:\n[MATH: 30]\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply [MATH: 3] by [MATH: 10] without any signs: [MATH: 3 \\cdot 10 = 30.] So, we get\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]\n\nContent:\nNote that multiplication is commutative, meaning it doesn't matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we're allowed to swap the numbers, which gives\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]"
      },
