    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nMultiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.\n\nContent:\nThe first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (product) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n[MATH: (\u2212)\u22c5(+) = (\u2212)or(+)\u22c5(\u2212) = (\u2212).]\n\nContent:\nFor instance, let's find out the value of [MATH: (-3) \\cdot 10.]\nHere, we have a product of a negative number [MATH: \\color{red}(-3)] and a positive one [MATH: {\\color{blue}(10)}.] So the product will be negative:\n[MATH: 30]\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply [MATH: 3] by [MATH: 10] without any signs: [MATH: 3 \\cdot 10 = 30.] So, we get\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]\n\nContent:\nNote that multiplication is commutative, meaning it doesn't matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we're allowed to swap the numbers, which gives\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{red} -30 }.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: (-5) \\cdot 2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe have a product of a negative number [MATH: ({\\color{red}-5})] and a positive number [MATH: ({\\color{blue}2}).] So the product will be negative:\n[MATH: 10]\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply [MATH: 5] by [MATH: 2] without any signs: [MATH: 5 \\cdot 2 = 10.] So, we get\n\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-5)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red}-10}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 10 \u22c5 (\u2212 2) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 5]\nb. [MATH: 8]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 20]\nd. [MATH: 20]\ne. [MATH: 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 3) \u22c5 2 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 6]\nc. [MATH: 5]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 5]\ne. [MATH: 6]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number With Decimals\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: (-20) \\cdot 1.2?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe have a product of a negative number [MATH: ({\\color{red}-20})] and a positive number [MATH: ({\\color{blue}1.2})]. So the product will be negative:\n[MATH: 24]\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply [MATH: 20] by [MATH: 1.2] without any signs: [MATH: 20 \\cdot 1.2 = 24.] So, we get\n\n[MATH: {\\color{red}(-20)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.2} = {\\color{red}-24}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 10 \u22c5 (\u2212 0.7) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 7.7]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 7]\nd. [MATH: 70]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 70]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (0.4) \u22c5 (\u2212 15) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 6]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 15.4]\nc. [MATH: 6]\nd. [MATH: 15.4]\ne. [MATH: 14.6]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 1.5) \u22c5 6 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7.5]\nb. [MATH: 9]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 9]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 4.5]\ne. [MATH: 4.5]"
